\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The chirality of a closed surface}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On the \vocabk{simplicial momentum grid}{fdip-013X} in dimension #{n\ge 2},
let #{\mathcal B(r)} be a Hermitian operator on one fixed finite-dimensional complex Hilbert space at every vertex #{r}. A \newvocab{closed surface} is an integer chain
#{\Sigma=\sum_t c_t t} of oriented grid triangles with #{\partial\Sigma=0}.
Thus the signed edge contributions from the triangle boundaries cancel. Let #{q\ge 1}, and suppose that at every vertex of every triangle of #{\Sigma} the
operator #{\mathcal B} is invertible with exactly #{q} negative eigenvalues. Choose an orthonormal
basis of each negative spectral subspace, as the columns of a matrix #{V_v}. For a triangle with
vertices #{v_0,v_1,v_2} put
##{z=\prod_{j=0}^{2}\det(V_{v_j}^\dagger V_{v_{j+1}}),\qquad v_3=v_0.}
Call it a \newvocab{regular triangle} if #{z\notin(-\infty,0]}. Its
\newvocab{flux} is the argument of #{z} in #{(-\pi,\pi)}. When every triangle of #{\Sigma} is
regular, the \newvocab{chirality} #{\operatorname{chir}(\Sigma)} is the sum of the terms
#{c_t\operatorname{flux}(t)}, divided by #{2\pi}.}
